WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.789 --> 00:00:06.000
From low-budget mysteries to ambitious blockbusters,
there's no bad Christopher Nolan movie.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:08.730
But which one reigns supreme as the very best?

00:00:08.830 --> 00:00:13.065
As Nolan's debut feature, Following is pretty
good in its own right.

00:00:13.065 --> 00:00:16.811
But more than anything else, it serves as
a testament to the types of movies that he

00:00:16.811 --> 00:00:18.180
would go on to make.

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:23.039
Clocking in at a breezy 70 minutes, it accomplishes
a lot in a tight little package.

00:00:23.039 --> 00:00:27.080
True to the title, the story kicks off with
a man who likes to follow people, which leads

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:28.850
him down a dark path.

00:00:28.850 --> 00:00:33.050
The narrative jumps around between the future
and the present, which is just one reason

00:00:33.050 --> 00:00:37.059
why looking back, it's clearly a harbinger
of the great films that Nolan would deliver

00:00:37.059 --> 00:00:39.750
in the years to come.

00:00:39.750 --> 00:00:44.329
Fresh off the groundbreaking Memento, Nolan
tried his hand at a suspenseful crime thriller

00:00:44.329 --> 00:00:49.044
with Insomnia, a remake of a 1997 Norwegian
film of the same name.

00:00:49.044 --> 00:00:53.500
Nolan uses the Alaskan setting to provide
some stunning imagery, and critics praised

00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:57.629
the end result as an "intricately constructed
thriller" and a tour de force for a young

00:00:57.629 --> 00:00:59.530
director early in his career.

00:00:59.530 --> 00:01:04.050
As he would so often later in the years to
come, the director also benefited from a talented

00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:10.040
marquee cast, including Al Pacino, Hilary
Swank, and Robin Williams, the latter of whom

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:14.035
delivered one of the most chilling performances
of his career.

00:01:14.035 --> 00:01:19.600
Nolan brought his trademark intelligence to
a period-set mystery with 2006's The Prestige,

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:23.439
and in the process, he pulled off one of the
most memorable twists in modern cinema.

00:01:23.439 --> 00:01:28.040
The story follows a pair of dueling magicians
played by Hugh Jackman and Christian Bale

00:01:28.040 --> 00:01:31.529
as they battle to one-up each other in 1890s
London.

00:01:31.529 --> 00:01:35.570
We won't spoil the big reveal, but we will
say that the deeper the film goes into these

00:01:35.570 --> 00:01:39.289
magicians' lives, the more delightfully deceptive
the story gets.

00:01:39.289 --> 00:01:44.725
Critics lauded the lead actor's performances,
as well as Nolan's deft execution of the big twists.

00:01:44.725 --> 00:01:48.890
And just in case you don't know, in magic,
the "prestige" refers to the big reveal of

00:01:48.890 --> 00:01:54.441
the illusion; with that in mind, let's just
say that this movie absolutely lives up to its title.

00:01:54.441 --> 00:01:55.711
"Abracadabra."

00:01:56.099 --> 00:02:00.969
The final entry in Nolan's Dark Knight trilogy
wasn't quite as strong as its predecessors,

00:02:00.969 --> 00:02:03.557
but it's still a fantastic film in its own
right.

00:02:03.557 --> 00:02:08.250
Nolan brought the story to a rousing conclusion
with massive action that turned the entire

00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:12.840
city of Gotham into a war zone under the control
of Bane, played by Tom Hardy.

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:17.500
Bane is a classic comics villain who became
one of the most imposing superhero bad guys

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:19.030
ever brought to the big screen.

00:02:19.030 --> 00:02:23.280
Anne Hathaway's Selina Kyle, meanwhile, offered
a wry spark and wit that the franchise had

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:24.870
previously been lacking.

00:02:24.870 --> 00:02:29.370
Although the Bat-formula was arguably getting
a bit stale at this point, Nolan still managed

00:02:29.370 --> 00:02:31.540
to go out with a bang.

00:02:31.540 --> 00:02:34.470
The year 2020 was definitely a strange one.

00:02:34.470 --> 00:02:38.660
With the world locked down because of the
COVID-19 pandemic, movie theaters suffered

00:02:38.660 --> 00:02:40.655
from the lack of an available audience.

00:02:40.655 --> 00:02:43.361
Tenet was released at the height of this uncertain
time.

00:02:43.361 --> 00:02:47.450
It was meant to be the major tentpole to get
everyone back to the movies, but it didn't

00:02:47.450 --> 00:02:51.179
quite work out that way, as many viewers waited
for the home release instead.

00:02:51.179 --> 00:02:57.300
Still, it managed to gross about $360 million
worldwide on a $205 million budget.

00:02:57.300 --> 00:03:02.230
In terms of the actual movie, Tenet is basically
Nolan's answer to Back to the Future, but

00:03:02.230 --> 00:03:03.940
with a spy thriller narrative.

00:03:03.940 --> 00:03:08.640
The ambitious story follows a character played
by John David Washington known only as the

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:12.920
Protagonist, who learns how to manipulate
time to stop future events from happening.

00:03:12.920 --> 00:03:17.000
In typical Nolan fashion, the film is like
an onion that needs its various layers peeled

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:19.200
back to reveal more about its message.

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:22.659
In this instance, it's humanity's relationship
with the concept of time.

00:03:22.659 --> 00:03:28.250
Also, since the director isn't the biggest
fan of CGI, he actually blew up a Boeing 747

00:03:28.250 --> 00:03:30.590
airliner for one of the action sequences.

00:03:30.590 --> 00:03:35.220
Ultimately, Tenet may go down as one of Nolan's
less-celebrated projects, mostly because of

00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:36.917
the bad timing of its release.

00:03:36.917 --> 00:03:40.950
But that should take nothing away from the
film itself, as it's an innovative effort

00:03:40.950 --> 00:03:44.849
that makes for a perfect double feature with
Inception.

00:03:44.849 --> 00:03:49.310
Nolan walked a tightrope with Interstellar,
telling a fairly grounded sci-fi story melded

00:03:49.310 --> 00:03:52.680
with a metaphysical message about the transcendent
power of love.

00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:57.560
It's a wildly ambitious saga spanning dimensions
with the fate of the human race at stake,

00:03:57.560 --> 00:03:59.739
and Nolan worked hard to get the science right.

00:03:59.739 --> 00:04:04.260
He pulled off stunning blockbuster spectacle
while also demonstrating how a black hole

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:08.939
affects the passage of time and utilizing
a wormhole to introduce some positively stunning

00:04:08.939 --> 00:04:09.950
alien worlds.

00:04:09.950 --> 00:04:14.181
Despite all that hard science, Nolan never
lost touch with the story's heart.

00:04:14.181 --> 00:04:18.799
At the middle of it all is a mission to save
humanity with poignant personal stakes, as

00:04:18.799 --> 00:04:23.090
Matthew McConaughey's character Joseph Cooper
copes with the fact that playing hero will

00:04:23.090 --> 00:04:25.276
likely cost him his life with his daughter.

00:04:25.591 --> 00:04:27.481
"Nice and easy, Mr. Cooper.

00:04:27.776 --> 00:04:30.447
Remember, you're no spring chicken anymore."

00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:34.930
The movie that first put Nolan on the map,
Memento is also a textbook example of what

00:04:34.930 --> 00:04:37.032
makes him such a great filmmaker.

00:04:37.032 --> 00:04:41.410
A noirish mystery and a gripping thriller
with the most unreliable of narrators, it

00:04:41.410 --> 00:04:45.800
forces the audience to piece together its
story one tiny detail at a time while searching

00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:50.350
for clues across the tattoo-covered skin of
its protagonist, played by Guy Pearce.

00:04:50.350 --> 00:04:54.100
The fact that Nolan both wrote and directed
Memento proved to Hollywood bigwigs that he

00:04:54.100 --> 00:04:58.620
had the chops to take big ideas and turn them
into something audiences would rave about.

00:04:58.620 --> 00:05:02.930
Critics called it a masterful execution of
a high-concept idea and one of the most original

00:05:02.930 --> 00:05:04.080
films in years.

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:06.187
"You don't want the truth.

00:05:06.187 --> 00:05:08.176
You make up your own truth."

00:05:08.509 --> 00:05:12.570
The Batman franchise was in shambles when
Nolan's pitch for a back-to-basics approach

00:05:12.570 --> 00:05:17.449
set the series back on track and reinvented
the entire superhero genre in the bargain.

00:05:17.449 --> 00:05:21.980
Batman Begins nailed the character's classic
origin story, breathing new life into a familiar

00:05:21.980 --> 00:05:26.040
tale with Christian Bale in the title role
and reinvigorating the character's appeal

00:05:26.040 --> 00:05:27.537
for a new generation.

00:05:27.537 --> 00:05:31.830
Entertaining as a comics adaptation or simply
as a straight-up action thriller grounded

00:05:31.830 --> 00:05:37.230
in surprisingly relatable human drama, Batman
Begins put Bruce Wayne back on top of the box office.

00:05:37.230 --> 00:05:41.229
And it also proved that Nolan had the chops
to helm blockbusters that transcend simple

00:05:41.229 --> 00:05:44.290
popcorn flick status.

00:05:44.290 --> 00:05:48.150
Inception would've easily been a mess in the
hands of a lesser director, but with Nolan

00:05:48.150 --> 00:05:50.680
at the helm, it's a mind-bending masterpiece.

00:05:50.680 --> 00:05:55.260
With an all-star cast led by Leonard DiCaprio,
this twisty thriller is so ambitious that

00:05:55.260 --> 00:05:59.280
it aims to bend reality to its will, and it
actually pulls off that lofty goal.

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:04.090
It's a heist film set in the subconscious,
and Nolan handles the dizzying layers of the

00:06:04.090 --> 00:06:08.650
story so deftly that it's basically impossible
not to be drawn in by the narrative even as

00:06:08.650 --> 00:06:10.509
you're trying to get your bearings.

00:06:10.509 --> 00:06:16.729
Hailed by critics as a smart, flashy visual
effects extravaganza, it remains a movie unlike any other.

00:06:16.729 --> 00:06:20.770
And Nolan is still one of the few directors
with enough clout to get a non-franchise picture

00:06:20.770 --> 00:06:23.590
with this kind of scope through the Hollywood
system at all.

00:06:23.590 --> 00:06:28.550
"Once an idea has taken hold of the brain,
it's almost impossible to eradicate.

00:06:28.550 --> 00:06:33.009
An Idea that is fully formed, fully understood,
that sticks."

00:06:33.009 --> 00:06:37.849
There's certainly no shortage of war movies
in Hollywood history, or World War II movies in particular.

00:06:37.849 --> 00:06:42.940
But Nolan still managed to give that mid-20th
century global conflict his own spin with Dunkirk.

00:06:42.940 --> 00:06:47.199
The story focuses on the daring real-life
evacuation of Allied troops from the titular

00:06:47.199 --> 00:06:50.550
French city before the area was overrun by
Nazis.

00:06:50.550 --> 00:06:54.770
Instead of relying on big action, Nolan used
a narrow perspective to present the crisis

00:06:54.770 --> 00:06:59.360
up close from the perspectives of the soldiers
awaiting rescue and the men braving near-certain

00:06:59.360 --> 00:07:00.789
death to save them.

00:07:00.789 --> 00:07:05.669
A talented ensemble lined up for Dunkirk,
including Kenneth Branagh and Nolan regulars

00:07:05.669 --> 00:07:07.694
like Cillian Murphy and Tom Hardy.

00:07:07.694 --> 00:07:13.642
Critics called the results masterful and a
fitting tribute to a harrowing yet inspirational mission.

00:07:13.938 --> 00:07:17.889
Nolan's Batman flicks are some of the best
superhero movies captured on film, and the

00:07:17.889 --> 00:07:22.450
absolute pinnacle is the second installment,
2008's The Dark Knight.

00:07:22.450 --> 00:07:26.440
After setting the stage with Batman Begins,
the director really started filling in his

00:07:26.440 --> 00:07:28.020
canvas with the sequel.

00:07:28.020 --> 00:07:32.949
The late Heath Ledger's mesmerizing, Oscar-winning
performance as the Joker is arguably the highlight

00:07:32.949 --> 00:07:34.729
of the entire franchise.

00:07:34.729 --> 00:07:40.199
The action is top-notch, the story is tight
and focused, and the visuals are stunning.

00:07:40.199 --> 00:07:43.819
Christian Bale had really settled into the
character at this point, and his command of

00:07:43.819 --> 00:07:47.392
the cowl made him a hero nearly as compelling
as his iconic foe.

00:07:47.392 --> 00:07:53.440
Critics lauded the movie as infinitely smart
and stylish, and the nearly universal acclaim is justified.

00:07:53.440 --> 00:07:57.510
The Dark Knight isn't just one of the best
superhero movies of the 21st century, it's

00:07:57.510 --> 00:07:59.600
one of the best movies, period.

00:07:59.600 --> 00:08:02.746
"See, I'm not a monster.

00:08:03.393 --> 00:08:05.366
I'm just ahead of the curve."

00:08:05.366 --> 00:08:09.569
It's pretty hilarious how the opening of the
portentous Oppenheimer became entangled with

00:08:09.569 --> 00:08:12.085
its bright pink release date partner, Barbie.

00:08:12.085 --> 00:08:16.229
But there's no need to understand that study
in contrasts to realize that Nolan's 12th

00:08:16.229 --> 00:08:18.699
feature may very well be his magnum opus.

00:08:18.699 --> 00:08:23.169
The biopic stars Cillian Murphy as J. Robert
Oppenheimer, who's often referred to as the

00:08:23.169 --> 00:08:24.759
father of the atomic bomb.

00:08:24.759 --> 00:08:29.150
It explores the nature of the man and his
team's groundbreaking scientific achievement,

00:08:29.150 --> 00:08:33.330
while also presenting the philosophical and
moral sides of the equation, as humanity is

00:08:33.330 --> 00:08:35.460
granted the power to destroy itself.

00:08:35.460 --> 00:08:40.340
In typical Nolan fashion, the film features
an all-star cast, including the likes of Emily

00:08:40.340 --> 00:08:46.707
Blunt, Florence Pugh, and Robert Downey Jr.,
who all turn in sensational and nuanced performances.

00:08:46.707 --> 00:08:50.940
This is a complex film about one of the most
harrowing times in modern history, and everyone

00:08:50.940 --> 00:08:53.275
treats it with the consideration it deserves.

00:08:53.275 --> 00:08:57.500
Nolan doesn't pull out the Hollywood gloss
and turn Oppenheimer into an unfortunate martyr

00:08:57.500 --> 00:09:00.920
or provide a traditional happy ending that
studios so often demand.

00:09:00.920 --> 00:09:05.450
Instead, he lets a thought-provoking question
ring long after the credits roll: namely,

00:09:05.450 --> 00:09:08.370
how far are we from total annihilation?

00:09:08.370 --> 00:09:12.770
From the pristine technical execution to the
deep thematic exploration, Oppenheimer is

00:09:12.770 --> 00:09:15.000
a singular cinematic achievement.

00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:19.390
It's not only Nolan's best, it's also likely
to be remembered as one of the greatest films

00:09:19.390 --> 00:09:20.390
of all time.

